Body: <p>Three major compilers (gcc, clang, MSVC) happily compile the following example (<a href="https://godbolt.org/#z:OYLghAFBqd5QCxAYwPYBMCmBRdBLAF1QCcAaPECAMzwBtMA7AQwFtMQByARg9KtQYEAysib0QXACx8BBAKoBnTAAUAHpwAMvAFYTStJg1DIApACYAQuYukl9ZATwDKjdAGFUtAK4sGIAOykrgAyeAyYAHI%2BAEaYxCAArKQADqgKhE4MHt6%2BASlpGQKh4VEssfFJdpgOmUIETMQE2T5%2BgVU1AnUNBMWRMXGJtvWNzbltwz1hfWUDCQCUtqhexMjsHAoExF4OANQAKpgbJhoAgib%2BVqc71zthBDfXB0cAzJdnpwD0Hw9e6UY3ymIqFiOxMzwAIrdBGC3l8HncHjtAcDMDDjmd/OC0ad0QiWEwwhA5ujzm8Hk97jdVNiTg9VAA6CmgiE7SQ087gjgLWicBK8PwcLSkVCcNzWaw7BRLFaYUFmZ48UgETRchYAaxAZkk9IAnHr9QaDQA2fScST8lXCzi8BQgDRKlULOCwJBoFjJOhxciUN0e%2BjxZAGIxcI0ae1YABueFWADU8JgAO4AeWSjE4ipotAIcVtEGiluiYQaAE907xC8xiMWk9FtNVldxeG62IIkwxaKXBbwsPijOIu6R8MR63gI4dLZhVNUvNmy%2BRBJgeQPaHhosQSx4sJbNngWGWFlQDMAFHHEym042ZIIRGJ2FIr/IlGpLbouPpDMZxZZ9KvbZAFqgySOAItocDai4js4ECuKMfhviEUylOUeipOkwFZJ4LQoQU6G9EhAxvu06FdCMmG5IREEdAwJGTCU/TxIREywXoGzdHh9ESAsUrLKsnGmhwfKkAKQoihwOyqAAHEaAC0RqSDsgYfjsIb0hoqk7BAuCECQcoKnMvANloczqpq2qGuZ%2Bomku5pCZaok2naDpdk6MCICgqDup6ZAUBAvpeSAx7MMkCgIKgBCDpgUaxvGyapgKGZ0NmxC5vmA4ViWc7pVWNZ1g4c7NowBBth2lo9h%2B/ZCkOI5jqBQqTtOs6Xnci6Wiua4bhgaxCjue6NgeR4njF57xbw/DXqI4j3mNj4qOoA66GY75GCgX42G1f5EsKQGZKB4H2OhLgMO4ZFwUER3sTMDH5GhmTMW%2BqGFAwF3IRR%2B21ExJ0sZRxETM9BFDN0d0A40f0MVx0q8Vw3K8haA6ieJUmyfJwDIMgGmbF4DBqnMGlaUQxC6VDBmOiZWq6hZ5lWWasMidatiOYZXKkM6bl%2Bf63q%2BR5foDBGyDJMkAD6EZcDqAsGNmGwC5JJqRtGmCnrFF4JVmOaUKlQpZZ2iqazl9b5R5LZFe2naVZgvbABV3Z4MONQ1ROU7IDOayKs1S5Cm165VpuXUGcQu77nwA0K8Nc7TTek3SNNiizS%2BIDPEtn6WNYP7RBtAHbSBdNEZkh3HTkp0IXRl3YTdAhAw9uGIRxr2QdRH3519b2dL9VfF4xgOfe3IOty94M8Xe0MCTTvDw9LOwsAovM7MLOr0uLhz3Jp%2BD44T%2BlOUZpNJNZw9WmB9P2ozxn8WYO/2evqqkGOyU55IQA" rel="nofollow noreferrer">godbolt</a>):</p>
<pre><code>struct Test
{
    // Member type `Test::Test` is supposedly introduced at this point
    // as an alias to `struct Test`, per
    // https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n4659/class#2

    // Shouldn't the following declaration be rejected since it redefines
    // the name `Test` in the same scope?
    // https://timsong-cpp.github.io/cppwp/n4659/basic.lookup#3
    int     Test;

    // Doing the same explicitly would be a compilation error, as expected:
    // using   Probe = int;
    // int     Probe;
};

int main()
{
    Test    x;
    x.Test = 4;
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Is the above example well-formed?</strong> and why. Why doesn't <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/injected-class-name" rel="nofollow noreferrer">injected-class-name</a> render the declaration of member <code>int Test;</code> invalid?</p>
<p><strike>Lookup rule [wrong reference to a newer standard] that might have made the example ill-formed with no diagnostic seem irrelevant, as no lookup happens here until the class is complete. What other clauses are relevant to this example?</strike></p>

Title: Is it possible to pass an AxiosInstance to openapi-generator generated TypeScript classes?.
Body: <p>How can i pass my AxiosInstance to classes generated by openapi-generator typescript-axios template?</p>
<p>I tried change prototype of BaseAPI class, but it doesn't work. And there are no places where i can pass my axios instance</p>
<pre><code>import { HotelsRestServiceApi, Configuration } from '../../generated/openapi';
import { BaseAPI } from '../../generated/openapi/base'; //Base api class generated by openapi
import { axiosClientNoAuth } from '../index'; //My AxiosInstance

const config = new Configuration({
  basePath: 'http://localhost:8080',
});

const baseApi = new BaseAPI(config, 'http://localhost:8080', axiosClientNoAuth);

const axios = new RestServiceApi(config);

