Title: Canvas drawing in a sandboxed iframe.
Body: <p>I'm trying to draw into a canvas that is located in a <code>sandbox</code>ed iframe. For some reason, this only works if <code>allow-scripts</code> is added to the sandbox white-list. I've tried finding any documentation on this limitation but couldn't. What's the reason? Is there a way to circumvent this? I cannot leave <code>allow-scripts</code> enabled because there the rest of the iframe content is based on untrusted input.</p>
<p>Canvas draw commands do seem pretty safe on the first glance but I must be missing some potential vector of attack so that browser vendors have decided to not allow doing so...</p>
<p>You can reproduce by accessing <code>index.html</code> on a server (e.g. <code>python3 -m http.server</code>). Opening index.html directly in the browser (as a file) won't work because of an invalid origin in that case.</p>
<p>Any pointers or even better, solutions, are greatly appreciated.</p>
<h2>index.html</h2>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;html lang=&quot;en&quot;&gt;

&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;script&gt;
        function updateCanvas() {
