Title: Vue.js 3 vs Vue.js 2 reactivity differences on simple example.
Body: <p>I have a project which i'm migrating from Vue2 to Vue3. I came upon this issue with dynamic changes to objects not reflecting in the template.</p>
<p>For example, i have this class <em>TestClass</em> which has a number property <em>duration</em>. In the constructor I'm incrementing this duration every second. This is the class:</p>
<pre><code>export default class TestClass {
  constructor(data) {
    this.name = data.name;
    this.duration = data.duration;

    setInterval(() =&gt; {
      ++this.duration;
    }, 1000);
  }
}
</code></pre>
<p>This is supposed to start the timer once the class is created with constructor call. However I don't see any changes when I use the class in a component, except from the initial setting of value.
This is my <em>TestComponent</em> in which I use the TestClass:</p>
<pre><code> export default {
  name: &quot;TestComponent&quot;,
  data() {
    return {
      testObject: new TestClass({ name: &quot;Test Class&quot;, duration: 5 }),
    };
  },
  computed: {
    time() {
      return this.testObject.duration;
    },
  },
};
&lt;template&gt;
  &lt;span&gt; Class time: {{ time }}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In contrast to Vue2 where changes would be displayed in the template, in Vue3 it always stays on inital value (this case 5). My question is, how to achieve same behavior as in Vue2 for this case? I'm aware that there are changes to reactivity between 2 and 3.</p>

Title: Dynamically importing dynamic components in Vue 2.7.
Body: <p>Please note that this is a legacy Vue <code>2.7</code> codebase running on webpack, so any Vue 3 or Vite related solutions unfortunately won't work for me.</p>
<p>I'm reworking an old navbar that is dynamically generated based on a JSON we receive from one of our endpoints. One of the things that I'm reworking is refactoring the icons from the old FontAwesome icons we were using to our homecooked icon library, where each icon is now a separate Vue component.</p>
<p>My issue is that this navbar will, at minimum, have 16 different items in it with 16 different icons. It's also possible for the user to customize their navbar and add/remove these 16 icons to their liking, though the icons will be limited to what our icon library offers.</p>
<p>So the solution here is to use the dynamic <code>&lt;component&gt;</code> tag, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;component :is=&quot;item.icon&quot; /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>The problem being that I have to import these icons and define them within <code>components: {}</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { MyIcon } from &quot;icons&quot;;

export default {
  components: { MyIcon }
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can see where the issue comes in, I have no idea which icons might be present in the navbar ahead of time. Importing the entire icon library is also out of the question, since we have somewhere around 800 icons and not all of them are currently used in the actual application, plus they're lazy-loaded and tree-shaken and all that good stuff.</p>
<p>Is there a way for me to iterate over the <code>items</code> array, and to dynamically import each icon that is defined from in there? The Object we receive from our endpoint looks something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>{
  icon: 'my-icon',
},
{
  icon: 'my-other-icon,
}
</code></pre>
<p>I think the solution here is using a dynamic <code>import()</code> in the <code>mounted</code> of the component (or some other hook) using the nav items, but I couldn't figure out a way of doing that without also importing every single icon (which is what I'm trying to avoid in the first place).</p>
<p>I've also tried a variant like below, but it doesn't really work with the way our Icon library is setup and leads to a number of hard to decipher webpack errors.</p>
<pre><code>const icon = import('myIconLibrary/${icon.type}/${icon.name}.vue')
</code></pre>

