Title: Is v-deep an anti-pattern in Vue.js?.
Body: <p>When styling a Vue.js component, you can use <code>::v-deep</code> to target CSS classes of nested components, e.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;template&gt;
  &lt;div class=&quot;parent&quot;&gt;
    &lt;Child /&gt; &lt;!-- contains .grandchild CSS class --&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/template&gt;

// ...

&lt;style lang=&quot;scss&quot; scoped&gt;
.parent {
  ::v-deep {
    .grandchild {
      display: block;
    }
  }
}
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Given that renaming or removing <code>.grandchild</code> could break consumers of <code>Child</code>, isn't using <code>v-deep</code> considered bad practice?</p>

