Title: google maps javascript api without browser.
Body: <p>I'm trying to get specific information from google maps which is only available through the JavaScript API. <strong>EDIT</strong>: <em>In particular I'm interested in the &quot;steps&quot;, &quot;legs&quot; and &quot;overview_path&quot; parts of the response. These data can only be received using the JavaScript API; cf. response of the (direct Directions) API call below to see absence of this information; <a href="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/distancematrix/json?origins=46.210553,6.1430196&amp;destinations=47.54758899999999,7.589662&amp;mode=transit&amp;transit_mode=rail&amp;key=YOUR_API_KEY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/distancematrix/json?origins=46.210553,6.1430196&amp;destinations=47.54758899999999,7.589662&amp;mode=transit&amp;transit_mode=rail&amp;key=YOUR_API_KEY</a></em></p>
<p>However, I don't need any kind of visualization or maps plot just the json response to my request.</p>
<p>So I'd like to run the request via NodeJS in my terminal with the code below which should output request and response to my terminal.</p>
<p>I’ve come accross the <code>googlemaps/js-api-loader</code> library which can be used for testing locally which is what I think I’m doing here (?):</p>
<p><strong>EDIT</strong>: <em>I've mistakenly used the DistanceMatrix instead of DirectionsService in my initial question. As pointed out @MrUpsidown in the comments for this I could google-maps-services-js NodeJS library. I updated the code and you could argue that again I can use this library, however, the info I want to have is not included in the response of the API as mentioned above.</em></p>
<pre><code>const { Loader } = require(&quot;@googlemaps/js-api-loader&quot;);

const loader = new Loader({
  apiKey: API_KEY,
  version: &quot;weekly&quot;
});

loader.load().then(() =&gt; {
  // initialize services
  const directionsService = new google.maps.DirectionsService()

  directionsService
  .route({
    origin: &quot;Genf&quot;,
    destination: &quot;Basel&quot;,
    travelMode: google.maps.TravelMode.TRANSIT,
  })
  .then((response) =&gt; {
    console.log(JSON.stringify(response, null, 2));
  })
});
</code></pre>
<p>This is where I'm stuck because running this in the terminal throws an error which I couldn’t fix so far.</p>
<pre><code>~$ node index.js
ReferenceError: window is not defined.
</code></pre>
<p>I’ve read that it might be possible to mock a window or a dom object, but I’m quite new to javascript and webdev so I’d be happy about some help here.</p>
<p>The best solution I've come up with so far is a kind of dirty workaround:  There is a <a href="https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/javascript/examples/distance-matrix" rel="nofollow noreferrer">google sample</a> which spins up a locally hosted website that can be opened in a browser calling localhost by running</p>
<pre><code>~$ npm i
~$ npm start
</code></pre>
<p>By modifying the request in this sample I’m able to get out the information I need. I automated this using pyppeteer as a headless browser to retrieve the response for further processing.</p>
<p>My hope is that it can be done without pyppeteer / puppeteer as this seems like a bit of overkill (and overengineering) due to my lack of understanding.</p>
<p>Thanks in advance for any tips and hints!</p>

