Title: Tab crashes when using window MutationObserver with Angular HostBinding.
Body: <p>I encountered a very weird bug when adding a MutationObserver to my application.
One of my Angular components is using HostBinding to the attr.style attribute of the element, this was working fine with the mutation observer I was injecting to the page (From another js file) up until a few days ago when I set the attributes flag to true for the mutation observer, then the tab crashed completely and got stuck in a loop.
I'm not sure why, the mutation observer shouldn't cause or trigger any new events, it should only observe, so I don't see how listening to the attributes of elements in the page can cause it, unless it does something more than just listening?</p>
<p>I set up a quick demo, there are 2 HostBindings, the first is irrelevant to the issue, the main issue is with binding to the attr.style.
If you want to recreate the bug, set the attributes flag to true for the observer.</p>
<p><a href="https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-hostbinding-class-demo-ud5m8g?file=src%2Fapp%2Fhello.component.ts" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://stackblitz.com/edit/angular-hostbinding-class-demo-ud5m8g?file=src%2Fapp%2Fhello.component.ts</a></p>
<p>Any help would be appreciated, thanks!</p>
<p>I tried to set the attributes flag to false and it did help, I just don't understand why. I need to keep using that flag. Another option, is to remove that binding and simply set that css rule manually later.</p>
<pre><code>@Component({
  selector: 'hello',
  template: `&lt;h1 &gt;This is a {{name}}!!!&lt;/h1&gt;
  &lt;button (click)='activate()'&gt;Activate&lt;/button&gt;`,
  styles: [
    `
   h1 { font-family: montserrat;}`,
  ],
})
export class HelloComponent implements OnInit {
  @Input() name: string;
  toggle: boolean;
  toogle: boolean;

  @HostBinding('class.Current')
  get t() {
    console.log('getter-toggle function called');
    return this.toggle;
  }
  @HostBinding('attr.style')
  public get cssVariables() {
    console.log('getter-cssRules function called');
    const cssVariables = `
    --sq-color: blue
    `;
    return this.sanitizer.bypassSecurityTrustStyle(cssVariables);
  }

  constructor(private sanitizer: DomSanitizer) {}

  activate() {
    this.toggle = !this.toggle;
    console.log(this.toggle);
  }

  ngOnInit() {
    let observer = new MutationObserver(this.logChanges);

    observer.observe(window.document.documentElement, {
      childList: true,
      subtree: true,
      attributes: false, // Switch this to true if you want to crash the tab
    });
  }

  logChanges(records, observer) {
    for (const record of records) {
      console.log(record);
    }
  }
}
</code></pre>

Title: Angular drag-and-drop directive adds characters to file extension.
Body: <p>There is a usual drag-and-drop directive in Angular. Everything works well, but it doesn't always work.
Sometimes in evt.dataTransfer.files[0]?.name comes either undefined or a changed file extension, for example, if the image1.jpeg file is dragged, then in the event the name is image1.jpeg0:-9:478:21</p>
<p>It is not clear what it is and where 0:-9:478:21 is added from. The last digits are always the same 478:21, only the first digits change. And this is only in Chrome. I haven't noticed this behavior in Firefox yet.</p>
<p>Working example here <a href="https://stackblitz.com/edit/file-upload-drag-n-drop-hye6lv?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://stackblitz.com/edit/file-upload-drag-n-drop-hye6lv?file=src%2Fapp%2Fapp.component.ts</a></p>
<pre><code>  @HostListener('drop', ['$event']) public onDrop(evt){
    evt.preventDefault();
