Title: c++ usual arithmetic conversions: cpp.sh vs godbolt different results - who is wrong?.
Body: <p>Here is a simple showcase of standard arithmetic conversion:</p>
<pre><code>// Example program
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

using namespace std;

int main()
{   
    unsigned long a = 0;
    int b = 1;
    long long c = -1;

    cout &lt;&lt; (c &lt; a*b) &lt;&lt; endl;
}
</code></pre>
<p>godbolt returns 0, cpp.sh returns 1.</p>
<p>Standard (<a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/usual_arithmetic_conversions" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/language/usual_arithmetic_conversions</a>) says:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Stage 4 ...</p>
<ul>
<li>If the integer conversion rank of U is greater than or equal to the    integer conversion rank of S, C is U.</li>
<li>Otherwise, if S can represent all of the values of U, C is S.</li>
<li>Otherwise, C is the unsigned integer type corresponding to S.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>Which compiler is <strong>wrong</strong> and <strong>why</strong>?</p>

