Title: .net 6 converts negative decimal number to currency different from .net48.
Body: <p>I have a method that converts decimal to string currency</p>
<pre><code>public static string ToCurrencyWithParentheses(this decimal initDecimal)
{
    return initDecimal.ToString(&quot;C&quot;, CultureInfo.GetCultureInfo(&quot;en-US&quot;));
}
</code></pre>
<p>We recently switched from .net48 to .net6 in a project and I noticed that the method began to work differently. On .net48 I would pass a negative number <code>-58.45</code> into the method and get <code>($58.45)</code>, but now on .net6 I get <code>-$58.45</code>.
Can anyone tell me how to fix the method without badcodding so that it processes the number correctly? Or is this a bug?</p>
<p>I couldn't google ready-made solutions. I tried using string.Format() instead of ToString() and stuff like that, but nothing works.</p>

