Title: C# System.Commandline: How do I add an argument to a command so that I can pass a value to it?.
Body: <p>The following simple <code>Program.cs</code> expects a single argument for a defined root command:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System.CommandLine;

var inputArgument = new Argument&lt;string&gt;(
  name: &quot;--input&quot;, 
  description: &quot;input any value and print it out&quot;);

var rootCommand = new RootCommand();
rootCommand.AddArgument(inputArgument);
rootCommand.SetHandler((inputArgumentValue) =&gt;
{
  Console.WriteLine($&quot;{inputArgumentValue}&quot;);
}, inputArgument);

rootCommand.Invoke(args);
</code></pre>
<p>I would expect calling this with the following parameter: <code>--input &quot;Hello World&quot;</code> to print out <em>Hello World</em> in the shell. However I get the following error:</p>
