Title: Handle settings of different types C++.
Body: <p>I don't really know how to explain the problem that I'm facing. This also makes it difficult for me to search the internet for answers. But I hope the issue will be clarified by the following code example that I made.</p>
<pre><code>struct ISetting
{
    virtual void* GetValue() = 0;
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct Setting : public ISetting
{
    T value;

    void* GetValue() 
    {
        return &amp;value;
    }
};

struct Settings
{
    Setting&lt;int&gt; setting1;
    Setting&lt;std::string&gt; setting2;
    std::vector&lt;ISetting*&gt; allSettings;

    Settings()
    {
        allSettings.push_back(&amp;setting1);
        allSettings.push_back(&amp;setting2);
    }

};

int main()
{
    Settings settings;
    settings.setting1.value = 5;
    settings.setting2.value = &quot;testing&quot;;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;settings.allSettings.size(); i++)
    {
        void* value = settings.allSettings[i]-&gt;GetValue();

        printf(&quot;value = ...\n&quot;);


    }        
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://cpp.sh/?source=%23include+%3Ciostream%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cfunctional%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Ctype_traits%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cstring%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cvector%3E%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+ISetting%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++virtual+void*+GetValue()+%3D+0%3B%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Atemplate%3Ctypename+T%3E%0D%0Astruct+Setting+%3A+public+ISetting%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++T+value%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++void*+GetValue()+%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++return+%26value%3B%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+Settings%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++Setting%3Cint%3E+setting1%3B%0D%0A++++Setting%3Cstd%3A%3Astring%3E+setting2%3B%0D%0A++++std%3A%3Avector%3CISetting*%3E+allSettings%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++Settings()%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++allSettings.push_back(%26setting1)%3B%0D%0A++++++++allSettings.push_back(%26setting2)%3B%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A++++%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Aint+main()%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++Settings+settings%3B%0D%0A++++settings.setting1.value+%3D+5%3B%0D%0A++++settings.setting2.value+%3D+%22testing%22%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++for(int+i%3D0%3B+i%3Csettings.allSettings.size()%3B+i%2B%2B)%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++void*+value+%3D+settings.allSettings%5Bi%5D-%3EGetValue()%3B%0D%0A++++++++%0D%0A++++++++printf(%22value+%3D+...%5Cn%22)%3B%0D%0A++++++++%0D%0A++++++++%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++++++%0D%0A++++return+0%3B%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0A" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Try this code</a></p>
<p>As you can see, I have a structure that contains settings. These settings can be of different types. Now I want to save those settings to flash (This runs on a micro controller) So I want to loop through all settings and write them accordingly. The problem is, I don't know the type of the setting since the type gets lost due to the interface. To simplify the issue I want to print the values of each setting to the output. Instead of writing to flash.</p>
<p>I did come up with one solution, but I'm not sure it this is the right way to go about it. The general idea is that the settings accept a visitor via its interface. The implemented setting then calls the appropiate function in the visitor with itself as varable. This visitor then knows what to do with this setting.</p>
<pre><code>template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct Setting;

struct ISettingVisitor
{
    virtual void Visit(Setting&lt;int&gt;* setting) = 0;
    virtual void Visit(Setting&lt;std::string&gt;* setting) = 0;
};

struct ISetting
{
    virtual void Accept(ISettingVisitor* handler) = 0;
};

template&lt;typename T&gt;
struct Setting : public ISetting
{
    T value;

    void Accept(ISettingVisitor* handler)
    {
        handler-&gt;Visit(this);
    }
};


struct Settings
{
    Setting&lt;int&gt; setting1;
    Setting&lt;std::string&gt; setting2;
    std::vector&lt;ISetting*&gt; allSettings;

    Settings()
    {
        allSettings.push_back(&amp;setting1);
        allSettings.push_back(&amp;setting2);
    }

};

struct SettingsPrinter : public ISettingVisitor
{
    void Visit(Setting&lt;int&gt;* setting)           { printf(&quot;value = %d\n&quot;, setting-&gt;value); }
    void Visit(Setting&lt;std::string&gt;* setting)   { printf(&quot;value = %s\n&quot;, setting-&gt;value.c_str()); }
};

int main()
{
    Settings settings;
    settings.setting1.value = 5;
    settings.setting2.value = &quot;testing&quot;;

    SettingsPrinter settingsPrinter;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;settings.allSettings.size(); i++)
    {
        settings.allSettings[i]-&gt;Accept(&amp;settingsPrinter);        
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://cpp.sh/?source=%23include+%3Ciostream%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cfunctional%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Ctype_traits%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cstring%3E%0D%0A%23include+%3Cvector%3E%0D%0A%0D%0Atemplate%3Ctypename+T%3E%0D%0Astruct+Setting%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+ISettingVisitor%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++virtual+void+Visit(Setting%3Cint%3E*+setting)+%3D+0%3B%0D%0A++++virtual+void+Visit(Setting%3Cstd%3A%3Astring%3E*+setting)+%3D+0%3B%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+ISetting%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++virtual+void+Accept(ISettingVisitor*+handler)+%3D+0%3B%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Atemplate%3Ctypename+T%3E%0D%0Astruct+Setting+%3A+public+ISetting%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++T+value%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++void+Accept(ISettingVisitor*+handler)%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++handler-%3EVisit(this)%3B%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+Settings%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++Setting%3Cint%3E+setting1%3B%0D%0A++++Setting%3Cstd%3A%3Astring%3E+setting2%3B%0D%0A++++std%3A%3Avector%3CISetting*%3E+allSettings%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++Settings()%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++allSettings.push_back(%26setting1)%3B%0D%0A++++++++allSettings.push_back(%26setting2)%3B%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A++++%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Astruct+SettingsPrinter+%3A+public+ISettingVisitor%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++void+Visit(Setting%3Cint%3E*+setting)+++++++++++%7B+printf(%22value+%3D+%25d%5Cn%22%2C+setting-%3Evalue)%3B+%7D%0D%0A++++void+Visit(Setting%3Cstd%3A%3Astring%3E*+setting)+++%7B+printf(%22value+%3D+%25s%5Cn%22%2C+setting-%3Evalue.c_str())%3B+%7D%0D%0A%7D%3B%0D%0A%0D%0Aint+main()%0D%0A%7B%0D%0A++++Settings+settings%3B%0D%0A++++settings.setting1.value+%3D+5%3B%0D%0A++++settings.setting2.value+%3D+%22testing%22%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++SettingsPrinter+settingsPrinter%3B%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++for(int+i%3D0%3B+i%3Csettings.allSettings.size()%3B+i%2B%2B)%0D%0A++++%7B%0D%0A++++++++settings.allSettings%5Bi%5D-%3EAccept(%26settingsPrinter)%3B++++++++%0D%0A++++%7D%0D%0A++++%0D%0A++++return+0%3B%0D%0A%7D%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0A%0D%0A" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Try this code</a></p>
<p>Lets say I want to export the settings to a JSON format. This will be something along the lines of:</p>
<pre><code>struct JSONExporter : public ISettingVisitor
{
    JSON* json = NULL;

    void Visit(Setting&lt;int&gt;* setting)           { json-&gt;AddInt(setting-&gt;name, setting-&gt;value); }
    void Visit(Setting&lt;std::string&gt;* setting)   { json-&gt;AddString(setting-&gt;name, setting-&gt;value); }

    std::string Export(Settings* settings)
    {
        json = new JSON();

        for(int i=0; i&lt;settings.allSettings.size(); i++)
        {
            settings.allSettings[i]-&gt;Accept(this);        
        }


        std::string result = json-&gt;ToString();
        delete json;
        return result;
    }    
};
</code></pre>
<p>I quess this can work, but I'm not sure if a more streamlined solution exists.</p>
<hr />
<p>Maybe a bit of a side question:</p>
<p>Is it possible to define the settings object in such a way that you cannot forget to add the settings to the list. Something along the lines of this:</p>
<pre><code>struct Settings
{
    MyIterableThing allSettings
    {
        Setting&lt;int&gt; setting1;
        Setting&lt;std::string&gt; setting2;
    };

    Settings()
    {

    }

};


void main()
{
    Settings settings;
    settings.setting1.value = 5;

    for (auto&amp; it : settings.allSettings) 
    {
        //Do something
    }
}
</code></pre>

Title: How to use an exported function in a script tag?.
