Title: Is creating a new scope in Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection thread safe?.
Body: <p>I need to start multiple tasks to process some data. And each task has to use a new scope. So currently I resolve a <code>IServiceScopeFactory</code> and then <code>CreateAsyncScope</code> from it inside the tasks:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Task Process(IServiceScopeFactory factory)
{
    await using var scope = factory.CreateAsyncScope();
    ...
}
...
Task.Run(() =&gt; Process(_serviceScopeFactory));
...

</code></pre>
<p>The question is - is this thread-safe? Or perhaps it is best to <code>CreateAsyncScope</code> in the main thread and pass it to the task manually?</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>Task Process(IServiceProvider serviceProvider)
{
    ...
}
...
await using var scope = _serviceScopeFactory.CreateAsyncScope();
Task.Run(() =&gt; Process(scope.ServiceProvider));
...
</code></pre>
<p>Being searching the internet but couldn't find any tips about this.</p>

Title: Bizarre concurrent behavior when I cast `ConcurrentDictionary` to an `IDictionary`.
Body: <p>I have the following test:</p>
<pre><code>using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Collections.Generic;
