Title: Pointer to &#39;this&#39; in an async method without using `fixed` or `GCHandle`.
Body: <p>Without thinking too hard about <em>why</em> you'd want to do this (assume crimes, if you like), I'd like to do something like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>struct Example
{
    // imagine there are members here

    public async Task DoThingAsync()
    {
        IntPtr p;
        unsafe
        {
            Example* ptr = &amp;this; // fails, complaining about unfixed expressions
            p = new IntPtr(ptr);
        }

        // I'd like p to still be valid here
        await Something.ElseAsync(p);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is there some way to do this? I know you can do it with locals (which are implicitly fixed, even in async methods), but <code>this</code> isn't a local. I know you can get this address of <code>this</code> inside a <code>fixed</code> statement, but it isn't possible to call <code>await</code> inside an <code>unsafe</code> block, so the flow of control will have exited the <code>fixed</code> block by the time we get to the async call and so the pointer could be dangling by then and everything will go sideways.</p>
<p>If we decide to make do with never modifying the struct instance via the pointer, then you can do <code>GCHandle.Alloc(this)</code> and then use <code>AddrOfPinnedObject()</code>, but that creates a boxed copy of the struct (which I'd like to avoid), and none of the other tricks I know for getting pointers to value types work on a <code>this</code> reference, and async methods can't take <code>in</code> or <code>ref</code> parameters so I can't work around it that way either... passing the struct instance in by value just results in a copy so it isn't any better than <code>GCHandle</code>.</p>
<p>I'm assuming the answer is &quot;<em>you can't do this, and you shouldn't even want to do this</em>&quot;, but in case anyone knows better: can you get a pointer to <code>this</code> in an <code>async</code> method, without using <code>fixed</code> or <code>GCHandle</code>? Or must I always assume that <code>this</code> could point to a totally different place across an <code>await</code> and outside of <code>GCHandle</code> or allocation on the unnmanaged heap, there's no way to pin it in place?</p>
<p>(I'm currently using dotnet 6/C#10, if that matters, but I'm happy for solutions and workarounds that require later versions)</p>

