Title: Display MongoDB collection data using PUG (Error: Array Undefined).
Body: <p>I am trying to display the data from my &quot;users&quot; collection in my MongoDB but receiving an undefined error with the array.</p>
<p>In my user controller file, I have imported express, express.router and my user model.</p>
<p>Here is the GET method I am using:</p>
<pre><code>router.get('/adminPanel', (req, res) =&gt; {
    try{
        const userArr = userModel.getAllUsers();
        res.render('adminPanel', { users: userArr });
    }
    catch(err){
        console.log(err);
    }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Here is the PUG code I am using to display the rendered data (adminPanel.pug):</p>
<pre><code>      table
        thead 
          tr 
            th User ID 
            th Username
            th Admin
        tbody
          if users 
            each user in users
              tr 
                td= user.id 
                td= user.username
                td= user.isAdmin
          else 
            tr 
              td(colspan='3') No users found
</code></pre>
<p>I have imported the controller and mounted it within my app.js file.</p>
<p>(NodeJS application using MVC architecture)</p>
<p>I've tried modifying my GET method to no avail.</p>
<p>I have ran the following test function within my user controller to debug:</p>
<pre><code>function test(){
    const users = userModel.getAllUsers();
    console.log(users);
}

test();
</code></pre>
<p>This did log the desired data in the console, there is just an issue with rendering it to the PUG file.</p>
<p>My only guess is that my PUG file is loading before the query and therefore reading an undefined array?</p>
<p><strong>EDIT</strong></p>
<p>getAllUsers() function defined in user model</p>
<pre><code>async function getAllUsers() {
    try {
        const users = await userModel.find().select(&quot;_id username isAdmin&quot;);
        console.log(users);
        return users;
    } catch (err) {
        console.log(err);
    }
}

</code></pre>
<p><strong>ANSWER</strong></p>
<p>Marked answer fixed 1 of two errors, I was calling an async function without &quot;await&quot; so it was not waiting for the returned promise.</p>
<p>The other error was within my mount, previously I had:</p>
<pre><code>app.use(&quot;/user&quot;, userController);
</code></pre>
<p>Which was the incorrect path to the page, fixed by changing to:</p>
<pre><code>app.use(&quot;/adminPanel&quot;, userController);
</code></pre>

