Title: Tailwind CSS Styles Not Applied in React App After Scraping Next.js Page.
Body: <p>I have a Next.js application (version 13) containing several components styled using Tailwind CSS. These components are then scraped from a CMS system written in React with Node.js and sanitized with <code>cheerio</code>. Post scraping, the resulting HTML content is sent to the React frontend and parsed using the <code>html-react-parser</code> library.</p>
<p>However, I am facing an issue: some Tailwind CSS styles are not applied in the React app, even though the CSS classes are retained intact. This inconsistency is evident when comparing the display of the same page in both the Next.js app and the React CMS.</p>
<p><strong>1. Next.js Components:</strong></p>
<pre><code>// Catalog component
&lt;section className='...'&gt;
  {cars.data?.map((car: Car) =&gt; (
    &lt;CarComponent key={car._id + ''} car={car} /&gt;
  ))}
&lt;/section&gt;

// Car component
&lt;div className='...' onClick={() =&gt; router.push(`${pathname}/${car._id}`)}&gt;
  &lt;DetailsComponent car={car} /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

// Details component
&lt;div className='...'&gt;
  // Image and details here...
&lt;/div&gt;

</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Scraping:</strong></p>
<pre><code>pageByUrl: async (_, { code }) =&gt; {
  // Scraping and sanitizing logic here...
  const html = await scrapeContent('http://localhost:3000' + url);
  const page = sanitizeHTML(html);
  return { page };
}

</code></pre>
<p><strong>3. React Frontend Parsing:</strong></p>
<pre><code>const EditableMain: FC = () =&gt; {
  // Parsing and rendering logic...
  const parsed = parse(page as string, options);
  return &lt;&gt;{html}&lt;/&gt;;
};

</code></pre>
<p><strong>4. Expected vs. Reality:</strong>
I expected the page to look identical in both the Next.js app and the React CMS, given that the classes remain the same. However, certain Tailwind CSS styles are missing in the React CMS version.</p>
<p><strong>5. Supporting Evidence:</strong>
I will provide two screenshots for comparison: one from the Next.js application and one from the React CMS. This will showcase the discrepancies in styling.</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/bYTo1.jpg" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Next.js App</a></p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/vUJ2B.jpg" rel="nofollow noreferrer">React CMS</a></p>

Title: Print sequence of tuples before returning a value for valid transactions.
Body: <p>You are given a matrix <code>V</code> of dimensions <code>m × n</code>, where each element represents the prices of m different vegetables seeds for n consecutive days in a produce market. Furthermore, you are given an integer <code>t (1 ≤ n)</code>, which is the max number of transaction you can perform in the matrix <code>V</code>. Now print a sequence of at most t transactions, each involving the purchase and sale of a vegetable seed, that yields the maximum profit after trading seeds. Note, you must buy a seed before selling, but you can only buy another (or same) seed on or after the sell day. If you can't get any profits from selling the seeds, print <code>(0,0,0,0)</code> else print a list of tuples like this <code>[(seed type index, buy day index, sell day index, profit yield)...]</code> before returning the total profit value.</p>
<pre><code>Example:
t = 3 (at most 3 transactions are allowed)
V = [[2, 6, 3],
     [4, 2, 8]]

Result:
Total profit yield returned is 10. Tuples: Buy seed type 0 on day 0 then sell 
it on day 1 which gives value of 4 (6-4). Then, buy seed type 1 on day 1 then
sell on day 2 which gives value of 6 (8-2). 

The final output should be:
[(0,0,1,4),(1,1,2,6)] 
10
</code></pre>
<p>Currently, code gives total profit yield value. I can't figure out how to print tuples to print all the transactions that give 10. I need to maintain the time complexity of O(m<em>n</em>t). Any guidance will be appreciated.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;tuple&gt;
#include &lt;algorithm&gt;
#include &lt;climits&gt;  

using namespace std;

int seeding(vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; V, int t) {

    int cols = V[0].size();
    int rows = V.size();

    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; profits_table(t + 1, vector&lt;int&gt;(cols, 0));

    vector&lt;std::tuple&lt;int, int, int, int&gt;&gt; tuple_result;
    tuple_result.push_back(std::make_tuple(0, 0, 0, 0));

    vector&lt;int&gt; max_diff(rows, 0);

    int tempMax = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i &lt; t + 1; i++) {


        for (int j = 0; j &lt; rows; j++) {
            max_diff[j] = -V[j][0];
        }


        for (int j = 1; j &lt; cols; j++) {

            for (int k = 0; k &lt; rows; k++) {


                tempMax = max(tempMax, V[k][j] + max_diff[k]);

                profits_table[i][j] = max(profits_table[i][j - 1], tempMax);

                max_diff[k] = max(max_diff[k], profits_table[i - 1][j] - V[k][j]);


                //for (const auto&amp; t : tuple_result)
                //    if (profits_table[i][j] &gt; get&lt;3&gt;(t)) {
                //        tuple_result.push_back(make_tuple(i , distance(V[i].begin(), find(V[i].begin(), V[i].end(), abs(max_diff[k]))),
                //                            j + 1, profits_table[i][j]));

                //    }


            }

            tempMax = 0;
        }
    }

    // cout &lt;&lt; &quot;[&quot;
    //for (const auto&amp; tt : tuple_result)
    //{
    //    cout &lt;&lt; &quot;(&quot; &lt;&lt; get&lt;0&gt;(tt) &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; get&lt;1&gt;(tt) &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt;
    //        get&lt;2&gt;(tt) &lt;&lt; &quot;, &quot; &lt;&lt; get&lt;3&gt;(tt) &lt;&lt; &quot;)&quot; &lt;&lt; endl;
    //}
    // cout &lt;&lt; &quot;]&quot;

    return profits_table[t][cols - 1];
}

int main()
{
    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; V = { {2, 6, 3},
                           {4, 2, 8 } };
    int k = 3;

    cout &lt;&lt; seeding(V, k) &lt;&lt; endl;
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>

Title: Interview Question &quot;An Optimized Solution for the given Problem&quot;.
Body: <p>Recently I was going through an interview and interviewer asked me very interesting question.</p>
<p>we are  given a integer matrix for &quot;N x M&quot; we can jump to any number which is strictly greater than  current number in the same row or column. we need to find what is the maximum number of cells we can visit. we can start from any point within the matrix.</p>
<p>Example :-</p>
<pre><code> {1, 0, 10}, 
 {3, 9, 7}, 
 {2, 6, 5}
</code></pre>
<p>Answer :- 6</p>
<p>Explanation :- In this case we have multiple correct paths one of the path is given below.</p>
<p>0 =&gt; 1 =&gt; 2 =&gt; 3 =&gt; 7 =&gt; 10</p>
<p>I write my solution for the given problem in Java</p>
<p>Time Complexity :-  N * M * (N + M)</p>
<p>Space Complexity :- N * M</p>
<p><a href="https://i.stack.imgur.com/c2bF1.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer">enter image description here</a></p>
<pre><code>static void longestIncreasingPath(int[][] matrix) {
    int n = matrix.length;
    int m = matrix[0].length;

    int[][] dp = new int[n][m];
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++)
        Arrays.fill(dp[i], -1);

    int answer = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; m; j++) {
            int length = findSolution(i, j, n, m, matrix, dp);
            answer = Math.max(answer, length);
        }
    }

    System.out.println(&quot;Longest increasing path length: &quot; + answer);
}

static int findSolution(int i, int j, int n, int m, int[][] matrix, int[][] dp) {
    if (dp[i][j] != -1) return dp[i][j];

    int res = 1;
    int cur = matrix[i][j];

    for (int newI = 0; newI &lt; n; newI++) {
        int newValue = matrix[newI][j];
        if (newValue &gt; cur) {
            int newRes = 1 + findSolution(newI, j, n, m, matrix, dp);
            res = Math.max(res, newRes);
        }
    }

    for (int newJ = 0; newJ &lt; m; newJ++) {
        int newValue = matrix[i][newJ];
        if (newValue &gt; cur) {
            int newRes = 1 + findSolution(i, newJ, n, m, matrix, dp);
            res = Math.max(res, newRes);
        }
    }

    return dp[i][j] = res;
}
</code></pre>
<p>and after this interviewer said Solution is Correct and asked me to more optimize it terms of Time-Complexity. That I couldn't able to do.</p>
<p>Can someone help me finding optimized solution for this problem?</p>
<p>When I was searching for optimized solution interviewer gave a hint of &quot;sorting&quot;.</p>
<p>If anyone want to play with my code
<a href="http://tpcg.io/_ERHJNY" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://tpcg.io/_ERHJNY</a></p>
<p>Few more test cases</p>
<p>[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0]] Output should be 1 in this case. Explanation: 0</p>
<p>[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, -1]] Output should be 2 in this case. Explanation: -1 =&gt; 0</p>
<p>[[0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 0], [0, 0, 1]] Output should be 2 in this case. Explanation: 0 =&gt; 1</p>

Title: Dynamic programming technique.
Body: <p>I have this problem that I'm trying to solve:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Given two numbers <code>K</code> and <code>S</code>, determine how many different values of <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, and <code>Z</code> exist such that <code>0 ≤ X, Y, Z ≤ K</code> and <code>X + Y + Z = S</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Now, what I did is a recursive solution that tries every possible value for each one of the three digits.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int count_sum(int k,int s,int sum=0,int limit=1,int counter=0)
{
    if(sum&gt;s)
        return 0;

    if(sum==s)
        return 1;

    if(limit&gt;3)
        return 0;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;=k; i++)
    {
        if(i&gt;s)
            break;
        counter=counter + count_sum(k,s,sum+i,limit+1);
    }
    return counter;
}
</code></pre>
<p>I know that this is <code>O(k^3)</code>, which is too high. I also know that there is a hack to solve this problem in <code>O(k^2)</code>. However, what I'm trying to do is optimize this solution with the technique of dynamic programming. I have tried to sketch a tree, but I couldn't figure out how to optimize it.</p>

Title: Finding the sum of Digits expressed as a string of Letters.
Body: <p>I have a string <code>str</code> of lowercase letters in the range <code>[a-z]</code>.
Few letters of the string when combined together, represent a digit from <code>[0-9]</code> i.e. &quot;zero&quot; = 0, &quot;one&quot; = 1, &quot;two&quot; = 2 and so on.</p>
<p>The characters in the string are jumbled. However, it is guaranteed that all the characters in the string can be used to form a set of digits. Some digits can be repeated as well. There are no invalid characters such as <code>a</code>, <code>p</code>, <code>q</code> etc.</p>
<p>My task is to find the sum of the digits represented by <code>str</code> .</p>
<p>Examples :<br />
<code>str</code> = &quot;oneeno&quot; should output <code>2 (1+1)</code><br />
<code>str</code> = &quot;onewtofoursevne&quot; should output <code>14 (1+2+4+7)</code></p>
<p>let <code>solve(s)</code> represent solving for a particular sub-string of <code>str</code> .<br />
i have an array of strings, <code>v</code> to store the spellings of the digits. v = {&quot;zero&quot;, &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, ... &quot;nine&quot;};</p>
<p>I will break down <code>s</code> and add the digit value, wherever I am able to find a set of characters representing a digit.</p>
<pre><code>if (contains(s, v[i]) == true)
    dp[s-v[i]] = i + solve(s-v[i])
</code></pre>
<p><code>s-v[i]</code> represents the set of characters in <code>v[i]</code> removed from <code>s</code> . As it is not possible to subtract strings in that manner, I substitute <code>s-v[i]</code> with <code>change(s, v[i])</code> (in my code).</p>
<p>This seems like a recursive function with multiple repeating sub-states.<br />
For example : <code>solve(s - v[1] - v[4] - v[7]) = solve(s - v[4] - v[1] - v[7])</code> .</p>
<p>I've used Top-Down Dynamic Programming for this where <code>dp[s-v[i]]</code> is the state of <code>s</code> when characters representing <code>v[i]</code>  are removed from it. Instead of removing characters, I simply replace the used characters with a <code>$</code> symbol. If the entire string has the <code>$</code> character, it means we've found a valid solution (which forms the base case).</p>
<p>As the strings are jumbled, I use a map of strings to store the sub-states and the strings are always sorted to prevent any redundancy.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;bits/stdc++.h&gt;
using namespace std;
using ll =  long long;

vector&lt;string&gt; v = {&quot;zero&quot;, &quot;one&quot;, &quot;two&quot;, &quot;three&quot;, &quot;four&quot;, &quot;five&quot;, &quot;six&quot;, &quot;seven&quot;, &quot;eight&quot;, &quot;nine&quot;};
map&lt;string, int&gt; dp;
bool b;

bool check(string str, int n) {
    string t = v[n];
    sort(t.begin(), t.end()); sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    int i=0, j=0, k=0;
    while(i &lt; t.size() &amp;&amp; j &lt; str.size()) {
        if(t[i] == str[j]) {
            ++i; ++j; ++k;
        }
        else ++j;
    }

    return (k == t.size()) ? true : false;   
}

string change(string str, int n) {
    string t = v[n];
    sort(t.begin(), t.end());
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    int i=0, j=0;
    while(i &lt; t.size() &amp;&amp; j &lt; str.size()) {
        if(t[i] == str[j]) {
            str[j] = '$';
            ++i; ++j;
        }
        else ++j;
    }
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());
    return str;
}

int solve(string str, map&lt;string, int&gt;&amp; dp) {

    if(str[str.size()-1] == '$') {
        b = true; // a solution exists
        return 0;
    }

    if(dp.find(str) != dp.end()) // if state is already computed
        return dp[str];

    for(int i=0; i&lt;10; i++) {
        if(check(str, i)) {
            return dp[change(str, i)] = i + solve(change(str, i), dp);
        }
    }

    return dp[str] = 0;
}

int main() {
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(0);
    cin.tie(0); cout.tie(0);

    #ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE
        freopen(&quot;input&quot;, &quot;r&quot;, stdin);
        freopen(&quot;output&quot;, &quot;w&quot;, stdout);
    #endif

    string str; cin &gt;&gt; str;
    sort(str.begin(), str.end());

    b = false; 

    int res = 0;

    for(int i=0; i&lt;10; i++) {
        if(check(str, i)) {
            dp[change(str, i)] = i + solve(change(str, i), dp);
        }
        if(b) { // valid solution found
            res = dp[change(str, i)];
            break; 
        }
    }

    cout &lt;&lt; res;    
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The code works alright for smaller strings :<br />
Example : <code>str = &quot;towsnveefourfive&quot;</code> yields <code>18 (2 + 7 + 4 + 5)</code><br />
However, for larger strings such as <code>str = &quot;towsnveefourfiveone&quot;</code> yields <code>0</code> instead of <code>19</code></p>
<p>What could be the error in my approach ?</p>

Title: Finding Maximal-Sum Positive Integer Subarray.
Body: <p>You are given an integer array consisting of N positive integers. You have to pick a subarray of length K. Subarray means a contiguous part of an array. You need to find the sum of the subarray with the maximal sum among all such subarrays.</p>
<p>Input should be given as follows. On the first line, N and K: the size of the array, the size of the subarray to choose. On the next line: N integers, Ai.
The output should be the answer, the sum of the subarray that has the maximal sum.</p>
<p>Constraints
<code>1 ≤ K ≤ N ≤ 1000</code> and <code>1 ≤ Ai ≤ 1000</code></p>
<p>I solved it like this but 2 tests give errors. What have I overlooked?</p>
<pre><code>#include&lt;iostream&gt;
using namespace std;
int main(){
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(NULL);
    cin.tie(NULL);
    int n,k;
    cin&gt;&gt;n&gt;&gt;k;
    int a,s[n],sum=0,ans=0,ans1;
    for(int i=0; i&lt;n; i++){
        cin&gt;&gt;a;
        sum+=a; 
        s[i]=sum;
    }
    if(k==n){
      cout&lt;&lt;sum&lt;&lt;endl;
      return 0;
    }
    for(int i=n-1; i&gt;=0; i--){
       if(i-k&gt;=0){
         ans1=s[i]-s[i-k];
       }
         ans=max(ans,ans1);
    }
    cout&lt;&lt;ans&lt;&lt;endl;
    return 0;}
</code></pre>
<p><a href="https://algoleague.com/problem/max-subarray-2/detail" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://algoleague.com/problem/max-subarray-2/detail</a></p>

Title: Memoization with recursion slower than just recursion.
Body: <p>I'm trying to understand time complexity-wise what to expect running fibonacci with recursion vs. recursion with memoization &amp; recursion.</p>
<p>I expect using only recursion - (2^n) - to be slower than with memoization <em><strong>and</strong></em> recursion - O(n). That is, memoization is one way to do dynamic programming, an optimization technique...so, I'd expect it to be faster.</p>
<p>First, run using recursion.</p>
<p>Time: <strong>10</strong>ms   leetcode:  beats 23%</p>
<pre><code>public int fib(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 2) {
        return n;
    }
    return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2)
}
</code></pre>
<p>Second, run with memoization <em><strong>and</strong></em> recursion.</p>
<p>Time: <strong>29</strong>ms leetcode: beats 13%</p>
<pre><code>public int fib(int n) {
    if (n &lt; 2) {
        return n;
    } 
    int[] dp = new int[n+1];
    if (dp[n] != 0){
        return dp[n];  
    } 
    int result = fib(n-1) + fib(n-2);
    dp[n] = result;
    return result;
}
</code></pre>
<p>You can see that using recursion alone is faster. Why?</p>

Title: SetTimeout Firing Twice.
Body: <p>This is the React code that I have for a page:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function ExportViewerPage(): JSX.Element {
  const [hasPrinted, setHasPrinted] = useState(false);

  if (!hasPrinted) {
    setHasPrinted(true);
    console.log(&quot;Inside if loop, hasPrinted: &quot;, hasPrinted)
    setTimeout(() =&gt; {
      console.log(&quot;Executing setTimeout: &quot;, hasPrinted)
    });
  }

  return (&lt;div&gt;Page&lt;/div&gt;)
}

export default ExportViewerPage;
</code></pre>
<p>Ultimately, the code should execute the code in the <code>setTimeout</code> function once. However, it actually executes twice as seen in the console here:</p>
<pre><code>Inside if loop, hasPrinted:  false
Executing setTimeout:  false
Executing setTimeout:  false
</code></pre>
<p>The weird thing is that the console log immediately before the <code>setTimeout</code> function only executes once, so the <code>setTimeout</code> is not being called twice and <code>hasPrinted</code> is getting set correctly. This is happening on both Chrome and Firefox.</p>
<p>I did notice that if I turn <code>reactStrictMode</code> off in the <code>next.config.js</code> file, the <code>setTimeout</code> will only fire once. But I am still confused as to why the <code>setTimeout</code> is firing twice with <code>reactStrictMode</code> on but the console log immediately above it is only logging once.</p>

Title: How can i Order the flow of execution of different promises in a correct way to get results?.
Body: <p>Flow of the promise chain execution</p>
<p><img src="https://i.stack.imgur.com/aJs2Z.png" alt="Flow of the promise chain execution" /></p>
<pre><code>const timeoutPromise = (interval, intervalName) =&gt; {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) =&gt; {
