Title: How does setTimeout achieve throttling in this MDN example?.
Body: <p>In the Mozilla Developer Network (MDN) documentation for the Element scroll event, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/scroll_event" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>, the following text is used to introduce the examples:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The following examples show how to use the scroll event with an event listener and with the onscroll event handler property. The setTimeout() method is used to throttle the event handler because scroll events can fire at a high rate.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Then there are two sections of code where setTimeout is used, for our purposes they are the same (one uses the scroll event and the other uses the onscroll event):</p>
<pre><code>element.addEventListener(&quot;scroll&quot;, (event) =&gt; {
  output.innerHTML = &quot;Scroll event fired!&quot;;
  setTimeout(() =&gt; {
    output.innerHTML = &quot;Waiting on scroll events...&quot;;
  }, 1000);
});
</code></pre>
<p>How does this use of setTimeout achieve <strong>throttling</strong>? (The text states that it does.)</p>
<p>As I read it, it looks to me like there is no reduction in the number or frequency of calls to the function reference passed to setTimeout, there is just a delay (as clearTimeout is never called), and therefore throttling has not been achieved, the whole sequence has just been moved one second later.</p>
<p>Do subsequent calls to setTimeout replace previously scheduled function calls if they have not been executed? That would explain it, but I did not find any mention of that in the <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/setTimeout" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MDN documentation for setTimeout</a>.</p>

